      "snippet": {
        "text": "This study examined formative assessment applications in English classroom instruction.To specifically understand formative assessment in K-12 education, we included (1) quantitative and qualitative empirical studies that assessed original data on formative assessment practices in English classroom instruction and (2) studies involving students engaged in K-12 education.Notably, some studies focusing on the English teacher population were included even if their teaching level was not explicitly stated in the articles.Studies were excluded if formative assessment was used in classes other than English, such as math, physics or other subjects.As this study was conducted in response to teachers' and researchers' need for information, reviews and theoretical studies were excluded.Given this framework, the following inclusion and exclusion criteria were applied:\n\nInclusion criteria:\n\n• The study was listed in either WoS or Scopus and published in a journal after undergoing peer review.\n\n• The research was conducted within the context of K-12 education.However, we were unable to confirm this requirement in some studies; these studies were also included.• The study focused on formative assessment in English classroom instruction.\n\n• The participants in the study were either receiving primary or secondary education.Some studies included teacher participants without specifying the quality of their work.studies in our research.At first, 1980 records were identified via database searches.Following the removal of duplicates, 1460 records remained.During the screening phase, by assessing titles and abstracts, we excluded 1452 records that were deemed irrelevant to our research subject.This led to the identification of 147 studies suitable for comprehensive analysis, from which 76 articles were subsequently excluded for the following reasons.\n\nExclusion criteria:\n\n• The study did not focus on learning-focused, classroom-based, sustainable or learning-oriented assessment or assessment as learning.\n\n• The method used in the study did not align with a formative assessment approach.Assessment was considered to be formative when teachers and students used information to adjust their teaching and learning methods (e.g.portfolio).• The study was not empirical.\n\n• The participants of this research did not study or work at a university.\n\n• The study did not analyse original data.\n\n• Assessment reform was not included in the study.\n\nIn summary, after the selection process, 40 articles were ultimately retained and incorporated into the analysis.",
