        }
      },
      "snippet": {
        "text": "Learning success is no longer measured solely through end results, such as test scores or student achievement reports. Instead, attention is increasingly directed to the learning process itself. In this context, formative assessment has become an important element in supporting the student-centered education paradigm. More than just an evaluation tool, formative assessment serves as a learning strategy that directly influences students' understanding, their engagement in the learning process, and the final results achieved (Montaño Oñate et al., 2024;Obro & Gift, 2022). Technological advances in the past few decades have opened up new opportunities to optimize the implementation of formative assessment. Digital tools such as online learning platforms and assessment apps allow teachers to provide real-time feedback and monitor student progress more efficiently. Technology also allows for the personalization of assessments, where tasks or questions are tailored to the ability level and needs of individual students (L. Yan & Singh, 2022). Formative assessment is defined as the process of using information about student learning to improve instruction and help students achieve learning goals (Weldmeskel & Dreyer, 2016;Xiao, 2017). The three main goals of formative assessment that they identified were to improve students' understanding of learning material, encourage students' active engagement in the learning process, and provide relevant and actionable feedback (Khursheed & Shahid, 2023;Wilkinson, 2024). This goal reflects the central role of formative assessment in supporting deeper and more meaningful learning. \n\nHowever, in the traditional education paradigm, the focus is often directed at summative evaluation, which is only carried out at the end of the learning process to assess student achievement. This approach has limitations in providing insight into students' learning processes, especially related to the challenges they face during the learning process. However, the adoption of technology in formative assessment is not without challenges. Limited access to technology in some schools and the lack of digital competence of teachers are significant obstacles. Therefore, teacher training is an essential factor to ensure effective implementation. In addition, the high workload of teachers often hinders the optimal implementation of formative assessment, considering that this approach requires great effort in designing tasks, providing feedback, and monitoring student progress.",
