      "snippet": {
        "text": "This scoping review demonstrates how teachers use digital technologies for school-based assessment of learning and assessment for learning, and in a small number of instances, for assessment as learning. The assessment uses are automated marking, provision of immediate feedback to students and teachers, and associated reduction in workload; provision of supplementary and new learning environments that include embedded formative assessment and feedback as well as mechanisms to track progress; and ways to collate and assess more diverse forms of evidence of learning. Digital technologies have also been used to support peer-feedback and self-reflection. As such, this review indicates that digital technologies can be used to support current school-based assessment practices, and game-based and virtual/augmented environments as well as ePortfolios can overcome the limitations of paper-based assessment. \n\nIn relation to the enactment of teacher-centred and student-centred pedagogies, this review indicates that digital technologies have been used to support associated assessment practices. Teacher-centred pedagogies are supported through improved efficiency and timeliness of feedback made possible by automated marking, feedback and progress tracking technologies. Game environments diversify feedback on drill and skill activities, and video recording supports the provision of feedback on performance-based activities. Student-centred pedagogies are supported through the functions of digital technologies that allow students to collect and curate diverse forms of evidence of learning as well as support students and teachers to assess and provide feedback on that evidence. Paperbased assessment techniques -paper-based exams and assignment responses -limit evidence of learning to modes that are supported by the medium, namely text and static images. ePortfolios remove this limitation. Additionally, open exploration allowed by game-based and virtual/augmented environments promotes student-centred learning opportunities. Collectively, pedagogy is supported by the efficiencies and diversifications made possible by school-based assessment uses of digital technologies. These uses further enable teacher agency in selection and application of approaches at the nexus of teaching, learning and assessment. \n\nThis scoping review has also identified some limitations. Firstly, papers about specific technologies are most commonly focussed on improving the technology industry, for example, validation of automated marking tools in web-based learning environments.",
