      "snippet": {
        "text": "Various conceptualizations of formative assessment (Black & Wiliam, 2009;Çekiç & Bakla, 2021) share the core idea of \"assessment functioning as a bridge between teaching and learning, helping teachers collect evidence about student achievement in order to adjust instruction to better meet student learning needs\" (Wiliam, 2007(Wiliam, , p. 1054)). The design of formative assessment tools is considered as promising to support teachers in adaptive teaching (Wiliam, 2007) and to widen the learning goals that get teachers' Extended author information available on the last page of the article attention in classrooms, because \"What you test is what you get\" (Burkhardt & Schoenfeld, 2018, p. 577). Although selected studies showed that these promises can be fulfilled effectively (Faber et al., 2017;Siemon, 2019), systematic topic-specific designs for all subject-matter topics and widespread implementations of formative assessment tools in subject-matter classrooms are still a desideratum (Burkhardt & Schoenfeld, 2018;Nortvedt & Buchholtz, 2018). \n\nDigital formative assessment (DFA) tools and platforms are hoped to contribute to more widespread implementations into classrooms, as the automated analysis can speed up teachers' interpretation processes, and immediate suggestions can be made available for adaptive learning opportunities (Gikandi et al., 2011;Looney, 2010). Whereas early digital assessment tools had serious technical restrictions (e.g. too restricted input formats for student responses), technological advances have allowed for increasingly sophisticated item formats, for example, on platforms with more analytical power such as the ItemBuilder (Kroehne, 2023;Olsher et al., 2023). However, despite these strong theoretical potentials of DFAs, recent surveys revealed that, with a few exceptions, the market of accessible DFA platforms hold promise with respect to automatic feedback but not with respect to widening the horizon of assessed learning goals to include higher-order learning goals (Thurm & Graevert, 2022).",
