        "text": "Students vary in their learning needs. It is widely recognised that it is important for teachers to deal with these variations by adapting instruction to students' needs (e.g. Bennett 2011; Reis and Renzulli 2010;Wiliam 2011). Several systematic meta-analyses have shown that the adaptation of instruction to the needs of students can lead to improved learning and achievement (Deunk et al. 2018;Hattie 2009;Hattie and Timperley 2007;Scheerens et al. 2007;Smale-Jacobse et al. 2019), although it has to be noted that these studies include a wide variety of differentiation approaches (e.g. between-class and within-class homogeneous ability grouping, computerised adaptive instruction, individualised learning) and that the effects found varied from no effects to moderate positive effects.\n\nIn order to adapt their instruction, teachers need to identify students' learning needs, and use this as a basis to make informed decisions about instructional adaptations. Identifying students' learning needs can be carried out by collecting data from students and analysing and interpreting these data (e.g. Feldman and Capobianco 2008;Gottheiner and Siegel 2012;Gulikers and Baartman 2017). Instructional adaptations can involve differentiation of content, process, and/or product (Tomlinson and Allan 2000). In other words, using data to adapt instruction to the needs of the students requires teachers to perform certain activities related to formative assessment (Gulikers and Baartman 2017;Sluijsmans, Joosten-ten Brinke, and van der Vleuten 2013;Visscher and Ehren 2011), as well as to differentiation (Prast et al. 2015;van Geel et al. 2019).\n\nFormative assessment has many different definitions (Sluijsmans, Joosten-ten Brinke, and van der Vleuten 2013;Wiliam 2011), but the crucial component is that teachers use the assessment results to adapt their instruction to the needs of the students . Formative assessment can be defined as a continuous and cyclical process of gathering evidence about student learning and using that evidence to guide the learning process of students through clear and detailed instruction, together with feedback (Black and Wiliam 1998). Formative assessment should lead to improved instruction (Marsh 2007;Sharkey",
