      "snippet": {
        "text": "Implementing formative assessment in EFL education faces multiple challenges.The literature reviewed in this study indicated that these challenges entail the individual characteristics of teachers and students as well as the broader educational system and cultural context.\n\nWhat underscores the importance of this finding is that teacher preparation and professional development are among the major challenges in implementing formative assessment.For instance, teachers who not have received professional training in formative assessment methods may lack the necessary knowledge and skills to effectively implement these strategies [18,28].Furthermore, teachers may face time and resource constraints that hinder the incorporation of formative assessment into their substantial teaching workload [41].\n\nStudent acceptance and engagement present additional challenges.Students may have reservations about formative assessment because of a lack of understanding or past learning experiences influencing their perceptions.For instance, students accustomed to traditional exams and summative assessment may experience discomfort with formative assessment approaches [18].Saleh Alharbi et al. [24] identified several additional challenges, such as computer skills, computer unavailability, Internet connectivity and time constraints.Considering these challenges, teachers believed that training students to help them overcome the specific challenges of online formative assessment is necessary.\n\nA macro view of the challenges of formative assessment includes the context of the culture and educational system.In some cultures, such as in China and Iran, test scores are considered the primary criterion for academic success, potentially overshadowing the importance of formative assessment [18].In other words, education systems that place excessive emphasis on standardised tests may limit the adoption of formative assessment methods.Consequently, teachers lack consistent assessment criteria, a long-term plan and support for designing formative assessment for their classes.In addition, class sizes may be too large to effectively adopt formative assessment, which can increase teachers' workload [39].\n\nTherefore, future studies should make multifaceted efforts to address these challenges.This includes enhancing teacher professional development, increasing student awareness of the value of formative assessment and adapting education systems and policies to better support students' learning and teachers' instruction.",
