      "snippet": {
        "text": "Formative assessment is a classroom practice that has been suggested as a possible way to support student motivation (e.g., Clark, 2012). It has been conceptualised and implemented in different ways (for a review of different conceptualisations, see Baird et al., 2014), but in the present study we will use the following often-cited definition of formative assessment by Black and Wiliam (2009) that incorporates several conceptualisations that have been suggested in the literature: \n\nPractice in a classroom is formative to the extent that evidence about student achievement is elicited, interpreted, and used by teachers, learners, or their peers, to make decisions about the next steps in instruction that are likely to be better, or better founded, than the decisions they would have taken in the absence of the evidence that was elicited (Black & Wiliam, 2009, p. 9). Thus, formative assessment is a teaching and learning practice. The term 'instruction' in the definition is meant to include both teaching and learning, including 'any activity that is intended to create learning' (Black & Wiliam, 2009, p.10), and the essence of the definition is that formative assessment is concerned with the creation of, and capitalization upon, 'moments of contingency' in instruction for the purpose of the regulation of learning processes' (Black & Wiliam, 2009, p.10). This means that formative assessment practices may have different features but are unified by the common core characteristics of teachers and/or students gathering information about the students' learning and adapting teaching and/or learning to the identified learning needs. For example, in one approach to formative assessment the teachers may gather evidence of student learning, and adapt feedback or the subsequent learning activities to the students' knowledge, skills and learning needs identified in these assessments. In two other approaches, the students take a more proactive role in the core formative assessment processes. They may support each other's learning through peer-assessment and subsequent peer feedback, the latter involving explanations and suggestions to peers on how they can act to reach their learning goals. They may also be proactive agents in the formative assessment processes as self-regulated learners who include self-assessment and subsequent actions to attain learning goals.",
