      "snippet": {
        "text": "Many studies have shown that the academic skills (e.g., reading, writing, mathematics) of students with disabilities often do not meet minimum standards (Gilmour et al., 2019;Graham et al., 2017;Schulte & Stevens, 2015). This is underscored by data from the National Assessment of Educational Progress (NAEP), which reveals that a significant proportion of students with disabilities underperform in key academic areas. For instance, 70% of fourth graders with reading disabilities and 77% of twelfth graders with mathematical disabilities fall below the basic proficiency level (National Assessment of Educational Progress [NAEP], 2019). This trend extends beyond traditional academic subjects, with notable challenges also observed in science and social studies among students with identified disabilities. \n\nThese dismal findings highlight the importance of investigations to determine which methods are most effective at helping improve the academic abilities of students with disabilities. Teaching (meta-) cognitive skills (e.g., planning, goal setting, organizing, and self-controlling) is central to the acquisition of more advanced competencies in literacy and numeracy, as these are needed for solving complex tasks with multiple steps and intermediate solutions (Peng & Fuchs, 2016). Many types of disabilities are associated with challenges in working memory, which manifest as slow and inaccurate processing, difficulties with planning and organizing, information filtering, and attention regulation (Graham et al., 2017;Peng & Fuchs, 2016). Therefore, it is important to effectively teach these students metacognitive skills to ensure that knowledge is stored in long-term memory. This is particularly important for understanding increasingly abstract concepts that are difficult for these students to retain (Dexter et al., 2011;Gage et al., 2014;Wagner et al., 2006;Zumbrunn & Bruning, 2013). \n\nOne method that has shown promising results in promoting conceptual 