      "snippet": {
        "text": "Assessment is often divided into the two categories: formative and summative [24]. Formative assessment is the type of assessment that aims to aid the students to identify knowledge gaps and learning opportunities and to direct their learning [24,84], often by providing timely feedback [34]. This can be done simply, for example, with online practice quizzes [3] and as part of a dialogue between the learner and tutor [61]. Formative assessment is often contrasted with summative assessment, which is used to evaluate and grade the students' knowledge or competency at a given time, often at the end of a course or a course module [24,84]. The terms \"formative\" and \"summative assessment\" were coined by Scriven [76], but the distinction between the two terms is not always clear. Formative assessment may encompass summative assessment but, in addition, requires feedback that indicates how to close an identified knowledge gap [84]. One possible distinction is that formative assessment is used by the learner, while summative assessment aims to judge the students' knowledge or competency. Summative assessment does not, per se, exclude feedback (for instance, a course grade could also be considered to be feedback), and the same assessment situation can be either summative or formative depending on what the student does with the feedback received [73]. Formative assessment is often perceived as useful by students [18], and one of the benefits of incorporating formative assessment is increased learner engagement [31]. From a teacher's perspective, formative assessment is used to improve teaching and learning by identifying learners' difficulties, while summative assessment is used to evaluate how well the students fulfill the intended learning outcomes [24]. This definition of formative and summative assessment guided the analysis in regards to RQ1 and is used throughout this article. How the assessment is structured within a course has also been shown to influence how the students study, where students have been found to focus on what they believe they are going to be summatively assessed on [62].",
