      "snippet": {
        "text": "Students' self-assessment plays a crucial role in developing both academic and professional competencies. Selfassessment is a multifaceted process engaging cognitive functions such as metacognition, self-regulation, and higher-order thinking, which are essential for critically evaluating and managing learning pathways [57]. These cognitive functions enable students to monitor their academic progress and empower them to take ownership of their educational experiences [58]. \n\nThe effectiveness of self-assessment is influenced by both internal factors, including personal beliefs and learning attitudes, and external factors, such as societal expectations and academic standards. Bourke and Mentis [59] assert that these values significantly shape how learners perceive and evaluate their competencies. Recent studies confirm that understanding these factors enhances the accuracy and efficacy of self-assessment practices in EFL settings [60]. \n\nEmpirical research highlights the positive impact of self-assessment on learning. For example, Papanthymou and Darra [61] found that self-assessment enhances students' learning abilities and social skills while fostering self-esteem and reducing anxiety. These effects are particularly relevant in EFL contexts, where self-assessment can boost learners' confidence in their language skills, leading to greater proficiency and increased WTC [62]. \n\nFurther research demonstrates that self-assessment improves English proficiency and builds confidence among EFL learners [19]. This reflective process encourages learners to monitor their progress, set realistic goals, and foster a sense of agency in their language-learning journey. Recent studies emphasize self-assessment as a skill that translates sustained effort into clearer progress appraisals and communicative engagement [32]. In the current study, self-assessment is examined as a mediating factor between L2 grit and motivation, affecting learners' WTC [63]. \n\nIn this research, self-assessment is defined as learners' ability to evaluate their language proficiency. This ability is closely linked to levels of L2 grit and motivation, both of which are critical for sustained engagement and longterm success in language learning. Through accurate selfassessment, learners identify areas for improvement and take proactive steps to address challenges [64]. Exploring these dynamics provides deeper insights into the psychological processes supporting successful language acquisition, insights that may guide the development of more effective pedagogical approaches [65].",
