      "snippet": {
        "text": "They may also be proactive agents in the formative assessment processes as self-regulated learners who include self-assessment and subsequent actions to attain learning goals. The teacher's role in accomplishing a formative assessment practice in which the students are proactive agents in the core formative assessment processes is to help them become motivated and proficient in carrying out these processes. The emphasis on, for example, planned assessment processes such as tests, or on information gathered about student learning from more informal day-to-day activities such as observations or dialogue may vary within and between approaches. Differences in the emphasis and implementation of feedback or adapted learning activities based on the assessments may also vary. For the formative assessment approaches above to be most efficient, the teacher may work together with the students to achieve a mutual understanding of the learning goals and the criteria for attaining them at different levels. Each one of the above-mentioned approaches may be considered to be a formative assessment practice in its own right, but they could also be combined to make up a more comprehensive multiapproach formative assessment practice in which the different approaches are used together as an inherent part of a unified formative assessment classroom practice. Wiliam and Thompson (2008) operationalized the definition by Black & Wiliam (2009) in a form that facilitates the learning, and practical use, of formative assessment in the classroom, and this framework was used in the analysis of the teacher practice in the present study. The framework describes the 'big idea' of using evidence about student learning to adjust instruction to better meet the students' learning needs, and the use of the following five key strategies (KS) that may be used to accomplish formative assessment practices (Wiliam & Thompson, 2008): \n\nand students as active agents in the big idea of using 