      "snippet": {
        "text": "The terms \"summative\" and \"formative\" assessment had been proposed by Michael Scriven in 1967 to differentiate between various roles that evaluation could play in the continuing improvement of the curriculum. In the subsequent years, Benjamin Bloom defined \"formative evaluation\" as the brief quizzes utilized by teachers and learners in the teaching-learning process as feedback or aid to judge the evaluation function (Wiliam & Thompson, 2017). In contrast to summative assessment, which evaluate student performance at the end of an instructional period, FA is designed to inform and guide instructional practices in real time. This approach not only helps educators identify students' strengths and weaknesses but also fosters a more interactive and engaging learning environment. Although both summative and formative assessments are essential in education, FA is often more appropriate for classroom learning due to its focus on ongoing feedback and student engagement (Black & Wiliam, 1998). \n\nFA can be defined as using assessment processes to support learning through different strategies like quizzes and observations (Wylie, 2020). Additionally, Wiliam (2017) described the concept of FA as the incorporation of all teaching activities implemented by teachers and instructional tasks performed by learners that equip the educational system with a type of feedback to modify the learning and teaching activities. In this regard, FAL includes the principal attitudes, skills, and knowledge that the teachers require to use the assessment process for refining pedagogical practice and improving the learning experience. \n\nThe theoretical framework of this study is grounded in the \"big idea\" theory of FA, as proposed by Black and Wiliam (2009). This framework offers a comprehensive lens for understanding the interactions between teachers and learners in the classroom, facilitating the effective implementation of FA practices. Five key strategies are conceptualized by this model:",
