        "text": "We began with all students whose first term of for-credit enrollment in the Ohio Community College system occurred between Fall 2007 and Winter 2011, who had not previously enrolled in any community college whether in-state or out-of-state (as determined via the NSC match), and who enrolled initially as undergraduates, excluding dual-enrolled high school students (N = 318,454). We further restricted the sample to students who had valid information on age at the time that they first enrolled in an Ohio community college and who were between the ages of 17 and 64 years when they first enrolled in an Ohio community college, excluding 1,885 students and reducing the sample to 316,569 students. We selected students entering between Fall 2007 and Winter 2011 because the data available to us allowed us to observe the graduation outcomes of these students across all types of institutions for at least six years, ensuring that even students enrolled on a part-time or intermittent basis had the opportunity to complete a postsecondary credential. \n\nAlthough our sample was limited to new community college students, these students were not necessarily new to college. About one-in-seven students attended a four-year institution prior to entering community college, and a small percentage of students-fewer than one-in-fifty-were concurrently enrolled in a four-year institution in their first term in community college. This inclusive approach to constructing the sample is an important feature of our focus on adult students. Adult students frequently have complex postsecondary histories. Students' postsecondary histories influence their goals and their use of community college to achieve those goals, resulting in marked variation in community college enrollment patterns. However, to ensure that the focus of this study remained on community college students, we excluded the 14,605 students who took a majority of their course credits in an Ohio public four-year instit