        "text": "Descriptions D. Educational context (Deeva et al., 2021) D1. Level (Deeva et al., 2021) Denotes the educational stage in which the automated feedback system is implemented. This includes settings ranging from primary education (early learning) to higher education (undergraduate and postgraduate studies) and adult education (professional development or continuing education programs). D2. Setting (Deeva et al., 2021)* Captures the instructional environment where feedback is delivered. Online settings provide fully digital learning experiences. Blended environments combine online and face-to-face instruction. In-class refers to traditional, instructor-led classroom settings, while lab-based formats emphasize hands-on, practical programming activities within controlled environments. D3. Teacher Implementation (Keuning et al., 2018)* Describes the role of feedback within instructional practice. Systems integrated into the course are embedded directly into the curriculum and teaching activities. Supplementary systems function as additional resources outside of formal instruction. Other implementations include experimental or informal instructional contexts. E. Evaluation (Deeva et al., 2021) E1. Evaluation method (Keuning et al., 2018) Defines the methodologies used to assess the effectiveness of the feedback systems. Empirical assessment involves systematic data collection and analysis to measure learning outcomes. Anecdotal evidence is based on subjective observations or reflections by educators or researchers. Analytical testing employs formal evaluation frameworks, including controlled experiments and usability testing, to evaluate system performance. E2. Impact on student performance/outcome (Cavalcanti et al., 2021)* Reflects the outcomes of feedback system usage on student learning. Positive impacts are associated with improved performance, engagement, or conceptual understanding. Negative impacts denote cases where feedback systems hinder learning due to factors such as over-reliance on automated responses or poorly designed feedback. Inconclusive outcomes arise from studies with mixed results or methodological limitations. E3. Impact on helping teachers (Cavalcanti et al., 2021)* \n\nExamines how feedback systems influence teaching practices. Improved instructional support indicates enhanced teaching effectiveness or workload reduction. Mixed outcomes involve both positive and negative effects, while unaddressed refers to studies that did not specifically evaluate the impact on teaching.",
